WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.001
As the fighting in Sudan

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.268
enters its six week,

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:02.869
it is forcing

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.104
hundreds of thousands of people

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:05.038
to flee their homes,

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.039
according to a new report

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:06.806
from the United Nations,

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:07.907
the International Organization

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:08.942
for Migration.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:09.876
More than 1 million

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:11.611
people have been displaced in Sudan.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:12.912
More than 300,000

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.547
have fled to neighboring countries.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.416
80,000 of them to Chad

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:17.317
or CNN correspondent

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.085
Larry Madowo reports.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.487
For us tonight, it's a report

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.922
you'll only see on CNN

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.793
The kids cry constantly.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:28.862
They'd always look weary of war.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:30.697
The pained faces here,

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.132
a reminder of the horrors

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:33.733
that drove them out of Sudan.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.602
To work at this refugee camp.

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:37.103
Across the border in Chad,

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:39.439
sadness stalks almost everyone.

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:40.573
As fighting

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:42.342
intensified in Sudan's western

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.710
Darfur region,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.912
they had to run or risk getting killed.

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:47.680
Kubra Abdalla left

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:48.748
so suddenly

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:50.784
that her son got lost in the chaos.

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:53.253
I know my brother is still back there.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.921
I heard he was injured.

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:56.623
I was forced to come to Chad to seek

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:57.290
safety.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.225
Would you go back to Sudan

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.794
and another game?

00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:01.795
No, no.

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:03.229
The only reason I will go back

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.298
is to bring my child and my brother here.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:06.232
There has been too much

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:07.600
insecurity for too long

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.737
because of decades of conflict in Sudan.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.072
Many of these refugees

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:14.107
had already been internally displaced

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.875
several times.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.744
Must Charlotte is 22,

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.412
but hasn't known a permanent home

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.081
for most of her life.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.982
I has highly engaged

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:23.550
I'm worried

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.852
about all the people we left behind,

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:26.920
especially my mother,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.922
who could not cross the border.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.090
I keep asking myself

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.591
how I can get out to Chad.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:32.192
I noticed that

00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:33.693
mostly women and children here.

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:36.029
Where are the men from? Sudan.

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:37.030
We know

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.731
the men told us to take the children

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:39.532
and cross the border

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:40.533
so they can stay behind

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:41.768
to defend themselves.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:44.037
And our property if necessary.

00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:44.671
The U.N.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:47.340
Refugee Agency says close to 90%

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.541
of new arrivals

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:49.576
in Chad from Sudan

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.511
are women and children.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:52.979
Many so traumatized,

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:53.480
but they will need

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.881
a lot of support to heal.

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.683
We had expected to meet refugees

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:57.851
as they arrived

00:01:57.851 --> 00:01:59.252
in the border town of Katherine,

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:00.620
right across from Sudan.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.222
But just before we arrived,

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:03.656
it was hit by a rocket.

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.492
That is why refugees are being moved away

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:06.726
from border towns

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:09.028
to places like this in Gaza.

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:10.497
CNN traveled with U.S.

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:11.364
aid administrators

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:13.733
Samantha Power to Eastern Chad.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:14.200
The U.S.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.202
is giving more than $100 million

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:17.470
to support the over

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.506
1 million people displaced by the war

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:20.840
across Sudan.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.642
And in neighboring countries.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:23.810
We met one woman whose

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.512
I had been gouged,

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:26.546
basically with somebody

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:27.647
just attacking her.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:28.248
And she's seeking

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:30.316
medical care here in Chad.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:31.885
Horrific violence

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:33.586
which triggers

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:34.621
for so many of these people.

00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:36.122
Also memories of previous

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.190
horrific violence.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.292
It's a full circle moment for her.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:41.161
She was in Chad in 2004

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.462
writing in The New Yorker

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:43.163
about Sudanese

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:44.731
civilians fleeing the Janjaweed

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:46.499
militia in Darfur.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:47.133
You talk to them,

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.835
you feel like you're in a time warp

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:50.970
because they're describing Janjaweed

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.438
coming in with their knives

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:53.840
and their machetes.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.075
Killing people, raping women.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.745
Is it's a real for you being here,

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.380
hearing these stories

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.047
when you heard them

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:03.116
20 years ago as a reporter?

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:06.319
Well, I feel lucky this time, at least,

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:07.921
to be working at USAID,

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:09.622
the big development humanitarian agency.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:10.990
At least there's something I can do.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.593
But fundamentally, there is no substitute

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.929
for the root causes getting addressed

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:17.730
for these two warring

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.732
generals to put their own

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:21.134
power grabs aside

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:23.236
and put the interests of these people

00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:24.337
who are fleeing, sometimes

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.472
for the fifth time in their lives.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:27.907
Chad, one of the world's

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.342
poorest countries,

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:30.843
had about 400,000

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:31.811
Sudanese refugees

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.146
before this latest surge.

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:34.914
We need to collectively, you know,

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:35.515
work with

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:36.916
all the

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:38.151
actors in support to

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:39.219
the government of Chad

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.353
to ensure that,

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:41.154
you know, resources

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:42.121
are mobilized

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:42.722
to address

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.891
the urgent needs of the of the refugees

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:46.025
today.

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:48.428
These are the innocent victims

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:50.830
of a deadly power struggle in Sudan.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.031
And the camera,

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:54.133
the poor and most vulnerable

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.668
who have no way to go.

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.003
Just another chapter

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.205
in a life of hardship.

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:02.141
And a son this Sudan conflict has quickly

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:03.109
spilled over

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:05.345
into a regional refugee crisis.

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:06.713
These latest statistics

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:08.748
from the UN are significant.

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.416
1.3 million people displaced

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:13.786
mostly in Sudan, but over 3000

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:15.622
have crossed into neighboring countries

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:17.690
like Egypt and Chad.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.258
The refugees

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:19.525
I met in eastern Chad

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.361
don't live in comfortable conditions.

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:22.295
The heat alone.

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:24.364
It's more than 110 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:25.598
They live in these

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.867
makeshift structures in the desert,

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:28.735
but they still prefer

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:31.804
that it's safer for them than in Darfur,

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:32.605
where they've come from.

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:34.073
So many of them have been displaced,

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.175
sometimes up to five times.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.844
And they don't care

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:39.279
which of the two warring

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:40.980
generals in Sudan wins this.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.782
They just want peace.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:43.650
They want the same things.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:44.484
You and I want

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:46.486
a chance at a dignified life.

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:47.954
And they will go back

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:49.522
for whoever can deliver that for them

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:50.423
Anderson.

00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:52.258
Larry, media reporting. Thank you.

